---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import RaidzMap from "../../components/RaidzMap.astro";
import WriteHole from "../../components/WriteHole.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

const lang = "en";
const r = room("raidz");
const V = "module/zfs/vdev_raidz.c";
const L = {
  maxParity: gh("include/sys/fs/zfs.h", 929),
  pqr: gh(V, 121, 123),
  pad: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 4718, 4729),
  nskip: gh(V, 699, 700),
  skipPlace: gh(V, 509, 580),
  skipIo: gh(V, 553, 572),
  dataLoop: gh(V, 548, 576),
  oneSector: gh(V, 2300, 2303),
  skipOpt: gh(V, 2481, 2506),
  genP: gh(V, 1141, 1156),
  startWrite: gh(V, 2443, 2462),
  readRow: gh(V, 2573, 2588),
  cksum: gh(V, 2767, 2772),
  readAll: gh(V, 3886, 3910),
  combrecCall: gh(V, 3959),
  order: gh(V, 3467, 3490),
  simulate: gh(V, 3283, 3299),
  einval: gh(V, 3385, 3392),
  parityChoice: gh(V, 2125, 2155),
  ecksum: gh(V, 3403, 3428),
  verify: gh(V, 3132, 3163),
  repair: gh(V, 3166, 3210),
  scrubRead: gh(V, 2580, 2582),
  state: gh(V, 3977, 3983),
  swap: gh(V, 725, 734),
  recurse: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 2248, 2255),
  visit: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 2440),
  window: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 4844, 4848),
  needIo: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 4878, 4880),
  dtlWindow: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 914, 915),
  expansion: gh(V, 152, 162),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="raidz"
  title="RAID-Z · Uberblok"
  description="How RAID-Z gives every block its own stripe, of variable width, so there is no write hole, and how it finds a disk that lies: interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)}</p>
      <h1>{r.title.en}</h1>
      <div class="prose problem">
        <p class="lead">
          Classic RAID-5 keeps a fixed stripe across its disks: some data and the parity of that data. To change one
          piece, it must write the new data and the new parity, in place, as two separate writes. A power failure between
          the two leaves a stripe whose parity no longer matches its data, and nothing on disk says so. When a disk later
          dies, its contents are rebuilt from that parity: wrong, without a word. This is the <em>write hole</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          A small write is costly too: to compute the new parity, RAID-5 must first read the rest of the stripe, or the
          old data and the old parity. RAID-Z does neither, because it never updates a stripe: every block gets a
          stripe of its own, as wide as the block needs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="map">
      <h2 id="map"><span class="n">1</span> One block, one stripe</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          When ZFS writes a block to a raidz vdev, <code>vdev_raidz_map_alloc</code> cuts that block, and that block alone,
          into columns, one per child disk. The <a href={L.pqr}>parity columns come first</a>, P, then Q and R for raidz2
          and raidz3 (<a href={L.maxParity}>3 at most</a>). The data columns follow, each one
          <a href={L.dataLoop}>a contiguous run of the block</a>: not one sector per disk in turn, as RAID is often drawn.
          Then come the skip sectors that round the allocation up. Pick a size, a parity, a number of disks and a sector
          size: the map and every number are what the code computes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <RaidzMap lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="hole">
      <h2 id="hole"><span class="n">2</span> No write hole</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The parity of a RAID-Z block is <a href={L.genP}>computed from its own data columns</a>, and
          <a href={L.startWrite}>every column is sent to its disk at once</a>: nothing old is read, nothing old is
          rewritten. The block goes to free space, as every block does in ZFS
          (<a href={roomUrl(lang, "copy-on-write")}>copy-on-write</a>), and the old version stays where it was until a
          transaction group that no longer needs it is committed by a single uberblock write
          (<a href={roomUrl(lang, "txg")}>transaction groups</a>). A power failure in the middle leaves a half-written
          stripe that nothing points to.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          That half of the argument is not in <code>vdev_raidz.c</code>: RAID-Z writes a whole stripe, and copy-on-write
          and the txg commit make sure no one reads it before it is whole.
        </p>
      </div>
      <WriteHole lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The idea, at three depths</h2>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one sentence</h3>
        <p class="lead prose">
          RAID-Z never updates a stripe: each block is written to free space as a stripe of its own, data and parity
          together, and the checksum in its parent tells which disk is lying when the block is read back.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one paragraph</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          A raidz vdev of <em>n</em> children with <em>p</em> parity is one address space, interleaved sector by sector
          across the children. A block of <em>s</em> sectors starts somewhere in it and takes
          ⌈<em>s</em> / (<em>n</em> − <em>p</em>)⌉ rows: its data in columns that each hold a contiguous run of the
          block, and <em>p</em> parity columns as long as the longest data column. A small block uses only as many columns
          as it needs: <a href={L.oneSector}>one sector of data always costs <em>p</em> + 1 sectors</a>, whatever the
          width. Every allocation is <a href={L.nskip}>rounded up to a multiple of <em>p</em> + 1</a> sectors, the size of the
          smallest block. On a read, ZFS reads the data
          columns only, and checks them against the checksum in the parent block pointer. If it fails and no disk
          reported an error, ZFS reads everything and tries, one combination after another, which disks to disbelieve,
          until the checksum verifies; then it rewrites the bad column.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In detail</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <strong>Cutting the block.</strong> By the time the I/O reaches the raidz vdev, its size has been
            <a href={L.pad}>padded to a whole sector</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/vdev_raidz.c"
          from={615}
          to={649}
          symbol="vdev_raidz_map_alloc"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 617,
              to: 623,
              text: "Everything is in sectors of <code>1 &lt;&lt; ashift</code>. <code>b</code> is where the block starts on the vdev, <code>s</code> its size. Sector <code>b</code> of the vdev is on child <code>b % dcols</code>, at sector <code>b / dcols</code> of that child.",
            },
            {
              from: 628,
              text: "One row: on a raidz that is not being expanded, the whole block is one stripe.",
            },
            {
              from: 634,
              to: 643,
              text: "<code>q</code> full rows of data, <code>r</code> sectors left over. The <code>bc</code> “big columns” get one more sector: the <code>r</code> data columns that hold the remainder, and the parity columns, which must be as long as the longest data column.",
            },
            {
              from: 649,
              text: "<code>tot</code>: data plus parity, the sectors actually written.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>Placing the columns.</strong> The same function then gives each column its child, its offset and its size.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/vdev_raidz.c"
          from={677}
          to={700}
          symbol="vdev_raidz_map_alloc"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 679,
              to: 686,
              text: "Column <code>c</code> is on child <code>f + c</code>. Past the last child it wraps to child 0, one sector further down: the block is a run of consecutive sectors of the vdev’s address space.",
            },
            {
              from: 688,
              to: 693,
              text: "A column past <code>acols</code> holds no data (a block narrower than the vdev); the first <code>bc</code> columns have <code>q + 1</code> sectors, the others <code>q</code>.",
            },
            {
              from: 699,
              to: 700,
              text: `The padding: <code>roundup(tot, nparity + 1) - tot</code> skip sectors, from column <code>bc</code> on. <a href="${L.skipPlace}">Where they land</a>: one after each short column, wrapping onto the parity columns if the stripe runs out of columns. They are written as zeros, <a href="${L.skipIo}">in the same I/O as their column</a>, or <a href="${L.skipOpt}">as an optional I/O of their own</a> for a column with no data, so that the disk queue can merge contiguous writes.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <strong>What the allocator reserves.</strong> The map writes <code>tot</code> sectors; the allocator has
            already reserved the rounded-up size, computed from the size alone.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/vdev_raidz.c"
          from={2305}
          to={2317}
          symbol="vdev_raidz_psize_to_asize"
          notes={[
            { from: 2315, text: "Data sectors: <code>psize</code> rounded up to a sector." },
            { from: 2316, text: "Plus <code>nparity</code> parity sectors for each row of <code>cols - nparity</code> data sectors that is started." },
            {
              from: 2317,
              text: "Rounded up to a multiple of <code>nparity + 1</code>. On a raidz2 of 12 disks with 4 KiB sectors, a 4 KiB block takes 12 KiB, an 8 KiB block 24 KiB (two skip sectors), a 40 KiB block 48 KiB: the figure computes any other.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            In raidz1, the parity column and the first data column <a href={L.swap}>swap children in every other
            megabyte</a> of the vdev, so that parity is not always on the same disk; the comment there says the benefit is
            doubtful but the on-disk format must keep it forever. Set the first sector <code>b</code> to 256 with 4 KiB
            sectors to see it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Reading it back.</strong> A normal read <a href={L.readRow}>asks only for the data columns</a>; parity is
            read when a disk is missing, or by a scrub or a resilver. The block is reassembled and
            <a href={L.cksum}>checked against the checksum in its parent block pointer</a>. A missing disk is an error ZFS
            knows about: its data column is rebuilt from the first parity column still valid, P before Q before R
            (<a href={L.parityChoice}>the choice</a>). But a disk can also return the wrong bytes and report success.
            Then the checksum fails and no disk is to blame. ZFS <a href={L.readAll}>reads every column</a>, and
            <a href={L.combrecCall}>calls <code>vdev_raidz_combrec</code></a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/vdev_raidz.c"
          from={3523}
          to={3558}
          symbol="vdev_raidz_combrec"
          notes={[
            { from: 3523, text: "Suppose one disk is lying, then two, up to <code>nparity</code>." },
            {
              from: 3532,
              to: 3536,
              text: `<code>n</code> logical children. Without expansion it is the number of disks, and logical child <em>i</em> is <a href="${L.simulate}">disk <em>i</em></a>.`,
            },
            {
              from: 3542,
              to: 3546,
              text: `The first combination, disks 0 to <code>num_failures - 1</code>; the loop after this excerpt walks through the others in <a href="${L.order}">the order the comment above the function lists</a>.`,
            },
            {
              from: 3549,
              to: 3558,
              text: `<code>raidz_reconstruct</code> treats those disks as dead, rebuilds their data columns from parity and asks the checksum again. <code>EINVAL</code>: with the errors already known, <a href="${L.einval}">more columns dead than parity</a>; try more failures. 0: the checksum verifies.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            The checksum is the oracle. Parity alone can rebuild a column ZFS knows to be missing, but it cannot say which
            of the columns it has is lying; the checksum, stored one level up in the tree, can. When a combination
            verifies, <a href={L.ecksum}>its data columns are marked as checksum errors</a>, the parity that was read but
            not used is <a href={L.verify}>regenerated and compared</a>, and <a href={L.repair}>the good data is written
            back</a> to every column found wrong. A disk that returns bad parity is not caught by a normal read, which does
            not read parity; <a href={L.scrubRead}>a scrub reads every column</a>. With more missing disks than parity, the
            vdev <a href={L.state}>cannot be opened</a>; with one up to <code>nparity</code>, it is degraded.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Resilver.</strong> When a disk comes back after missing some writes, ZFS does not copy the whole disk.
            A resilver walks the tree of block pointers, <a href={L.recurse}>indirect block by indirect block</a>, and
            <a href={L.visit}>visits each block</a>. It skips the blocks <a href={L.window}>born outside the range of
            txgs</a> that <a href={L.dtlWindow}>some disk missed</a>, then <a href={L.needIo}>asks the vdev</a> whether
            this block touches such a disk.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/vdev_raidz.c"
          from={4011}
          to={4037}
          symbol="vdev_raidz_need_resilver"
          notes={[
            { from: 4018, to: 4019, text: "The block was not born while a disk was missing writes: nothing to do." },
            { from: 4021, to: 4022, text: "A block with at least as many columns as disks touches them all." },
            {
              from: 4024,
              to: 4035,
              text: "Otherwise, only if one of the <code>s + nparity</code> disks from the first column on missed writes. In the figure, set a disk to missing and read: the result says whether a resilver would rewrite this block.",
            },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure runs the arithmetic of <code>vdev_raidz_map_alloc</code> and <code>vdev_raidz_psize_to_asize</code>
        as the code writes it, so every layout and every number is the code’s. The read follows the code’s decisions
        (which columns are read, which parity rebuilds what, the order of the combinations, what is written back), but
        tracks only whether each column is right or wrong, not its bytes: a rebuild is right exactly when every column it
        reads is right. It leaves out disks that missed writes (read as stale, not missing), slow disks skipped on
        purpose, gang blocks, the scrub path, and dRAID. Blocks are limited to 128 KiB and 16 disks so the map stays
        legible. RAID-Z expansion, which <a href={L.expansion}>adds a disk to an existing raidz</a> and gives older blocks
        maps of several rows, is left for another time.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="raidz" />
</Base>

